WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:06.800
When knitting in stitch pattern,
it's important to consider the end needles.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.080
On this manual machine, I've selected some
needles and I set the carriage to slip.

3
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.800
You can see the large floats that are
formed.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.800
But notice that because the end needle was
left in B or working position,

5
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.600
when I knit across, the end
needle didn't knit.

6
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.400
And when I knit back, the working yarn will pass

7
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.760
back, distorting that slipped
stitch and forming a hole.

8
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.080
To complete the slip stitch,

9
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.800
I need to make sure that the
machine knits the end needle.

10
00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.440
I'm manually knitting this end stitch.
And this is what I want the row to look

11
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.240
like; the float going all the way
to the edge of the knitting.

12
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.800
Since this is a manual machine,
I'll pull out that end needle to middle

13
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.960
position along with the rest
of the pattern needles.

14
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.560
Now, when I knit across the needle,
will knit-along with all the other needles

15
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:28.440
that are in middle position
and the needles and B will slip.

16
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.280
By selecting that end needle,

17
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.600
the slip float travels across the row
and all the stitches form properly.

18
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.840
On some automatic patterning machines,
you can control the end needle selection

19
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.920
and you can program your pattern

20
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.400
for end needle selection as well.
But no matter what machine you have,

21
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.520
be aware of what's happening
along the edge of your knitting.

